    {
      "label": "reviewed actual Windows clock precision regression",
      "local_commit": "98e627e8f55dfd781d2b43943557d5dbd2ad6cbe",
      "tree": "639abd80146e2f65ce3879c030c3b9f4592e8ae8",
      "ordered_parents": [
        "674102bd42ee11807d941e0f91b56dad63c2e945"
      ],
      "message": "test: respect native SystemTime precision at the Unix epoch\n\nThe actual Windows run quantizes a one-nanosecond SystemTime subtraction\nback to the epoch. Preserve the Timestamp nanosecond representation\nassertions, add a negative 100-nanosecond roundtrip, and keep unconditional\none-nanosecond coverage against the actual standard clock duration.\n\nRetain every following timestamp test and all five prior compiler-repair\nregressions. No production conversion or test skip is changed. Native rerun\nremains pending on the exact composed candidate.\n",
      "raw_sha256": "97f0b7ea0027a22505338225c70edd96548b972a9567fb871bb66829404d02a4",
      "base_parent": "674102bd42ee11807d941e0f91b56dad63c2e945",
